{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## CRITICAL constraint for the FINAL SWEEP — the keep-one rule must INVERT\n\nOperator instruction: *\"Be cautious not to remove the comment with the ID when you perform the\nfinal cleanup.\"* Recording the full reasoning, because getting this backwards re-creates the\noriginal incident.\n\n### Why the rule flips after the bridge is re-enabled\n\n`_record_comment_id` (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:311-333`) records a comment id **only on a\nsuccessful new post**. It does not backfill ids for comments already in Jira. So on the first pass\nafter re-enabling:\n\n1. existing comments have no recorded id → the PRIMARY id-identity skip cannot fire for them;\n2. truncated bodies cannot match the SECONDARY body-equality skip;\n3. the bridge posts a fresh copy and **records THAT copy's id** in `comment_ids`.\n\nThe id therefore points at the **NEWEST** copy — the one the bridge just created.\n\nPhase 1 and phase 2 both use \"keep the OLDEST copy of each distinct body\". Applying that same rule\nto the final sweep would delete the newest copy — **the one the id points at**. The result is a\n`comment_ids` entry referencing a deleted comment: the primary skip matches a comment that no\nlonger exists, so the bridge posts again, records a new id, and the cycle repeats. That is the\noriginal defect, re-created by the cleanup meant to finish it.\n\n### The rule for the final sweep\n\nFor each distinct normalized body, **keep the copy whose id appears in the binding store's\n`comment_ids` map**. Delete the others. Only where NO copy is id-referenced does the oldest-copy\nrule apply.\n\nRead the authoritative map from `git show origin/tickets:.bridge_state/bindings.json` at sweep\ntime — not from a cached copy, since the bridge writes it on every pass.\n\n### Current state — verified, so THIS phase is unaffected\n\n```\nbindings: 2475   comment_ids: 0\n```\n\nThe map is still empty (the bridge has not run since the fix merged), so **no comment is\nid-referenced right now** and phase 2's oldest-keep rule is safe as written. The inversion applies\nonly to the sweep that follows the first re-enabled pass.\n\n### Sequencing, as approved\n\nOperator selected option (A): phase 2 now → re-enable → final sweep for whatever the first pass\nadds. The expected re-post is ONE copy per distinct truncated comment, not a re-inflation to 836,\nbecause the bridge posts per local comment rather than per existing duplicate. **That figure is\ninferred, not measured** — the first pass should be counted rather than assumed, and a material\ndivergence from it is a signal to stop and re-examine rather than to proceed with the sweep."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787272897490842001,"uuid":"c3bb0cd3-c86f-47b1-a998-68be4556d28f"}